the user and are not distributed here.

Runtime dependencies (not bundled)
----------------------------------

  PyMuPDF / pymupdf4llm — Artifex Software, AGPL-3.0
      Used for page-accurate extraction, heading-preserving Markdown, and the
      text-layer probe. Our code calls it; we do not ship or modify it.

  markitdown — Microsoft, MIT
      Used for non-PDF input and for flat text conversion.

  Node.js built-ins only on the JavaScript side — this package has no npm
  dependencies.

Prior art and inspiration
-------------------------

No code was copied from these. They informed framing and content:

  Anthropic "skills" repository, skills/pdf (source-available, proprietary)
      Its skill-description phrasing influenced how this skill advertises its
      triggers. Only the frontmatter and overview of that file were read; none
      of its text or code appears here.

  Microsoft markitdown README (MIT)
      Its explicit statement that output is meant for text-analysis tools rather
      than high-fidelity human consumption shaped references/quality.md.

  REA (rea-agents, morluto/rea) — reverse-engineering skill
      Its practice of declaring analytical limitations inline, rather than
      letting results speak for themselves, shaped the "tell the truth about
      what the conversion could not do" rule.

  DSH bundle conventions
      The package layout (package.json `dsh.bundle.patch` → cordis.patch.yml →
      a plugin that injects `ctx.skills`) is the documented DeepSeek Harness
      plugin shape, not this package's invention. An earlier draft of
      lib/startup.js was adapted from dsh-academic-research-skills
      (CC-BY-NC-4.0); it was rewritten from scratch with a different
      implementation so that this package can be MIT without a licence
      conflict. No line of that package remains.

